  "nl_text": "The database schema named \"schemabench_0107\" contains 8 tables. The tables are described as follows:\n1. `account`: This table contains information about bank accounts. The primary key is `account_id`, which is an integer. The `district_id` is an integer and represents the district where the account is located. The `frequency` and `date` fields are strings and represent the frequency of transactions and the date of the last transaction, respectively. The `district_id` field is a foreign key referencing the `district` table.\n2. `card`: This table contains information about bank cards. The primary key is `card_id`, which is an integer. The `disp_id` is an integer and represents the disposition id. The `type` and `issued` fields are strings and represent the type of the card and the date it was issued, respectively. The `disp_id` field is a foreign key referencing the `disp` table.\n3. `client`: This table contains information about bank clients. The primary key is `client_id`, which is an integer. The `gender`, `birth_date`, and `district_id` fields are strings and represent the gender of the client, the birth date of the client, and the district where the client is located, respectively. The `district_id` field is a foreign key referencing the `district` table.\n4. `disp`: This table contains information about bank dispositions. The primary key is `disp_id`, which is an integer. The `client_id` and `account_id` fields are integers and represent the client id and account id, respectively. The `type` field is a string and represents the type of disposition. The `account_id` and `client_id` fields are foreign keys referencing the `account` and `client` tables, respectively.\n5. `district`: This table contains information about districts. The primary key is `district_id`, which is an integer. The fields `A2` to `A16` are strings and represent various attributes of the district.\n6. `loan`: This table contains information about bank loans. The primary key is `loan_id`, which is an integer. The `account_id`, `date`, `amount`, `duration`, `payments`, and `status` fields are strings and represent the account id, the date of the loan, the amount of the loan, the duration of the loan, the payments made, and the status of the loan, respectively. The `account_id` field is a foreign key referencing the `account` table.\n7. `order`: This table contains information about bank orders. The primary key is `order_id`, which is an integer. The `account_id`, `bank_to`, `account_to`, `amount`, `k_symbol` fields are strings and represent the account id, the bank to which the order is made, the account to which the order is made, the amount of the order, and the symbol of the order, respectively. The `account_id` field is a foreign key referencing the `account` table.\n8. `trans`: This table contains information about bank transactions. The primary key is `trans_id`, which is an integer. The `account_id`, `date`, `type`, `operation`, `amount`, `balance`, `k_symbol`, `bank`, `account` fields are strings and represent the account id, the date of the transaction, the type of the transaction, the operation of the transaction, the amount of the transaction, the balance of the account after the transaction, the symbol of the transaction, the bank of the transaction, and the account of the transaction, respectively. The `account_id` field is a foreign key referencing the `account` table.\nAll tables allow null values except for the ones that have not specified not to allow null. The foreign keys have on delete no action and on update no action rules, meaning that if a record in the parent table is deleted or updated, no action will be taken on the corresponding records in the child table.",
  "gold_question": "Background / Context\nThe project involves utilizing the PKDD'99 Financial dataset, which contains information on 606 successful loans and 76 unsuccessful loans, along with associated account and transaction details. The dataset aims to assist in understanding and predicting loan outcomes based on historical financial data at the starting point of the loan.\n\n Objectives\nThe primary objective is to develop a system capable of predicting the outcome of a loan (successful or unsuccessful) at the time of loan initiation. This predictive capability will help financial institutions assess risks and make informed lending decisions.\n\n Stakeholders & Users\n- Financial Analysts: Use the system to analyze loan data and predict outcomes.\n- Loan Officers: Aid in decision-making for approving or rejecting loan applications.\n- Data Scientists & Engineers: Implement and maintain prediction models and system infrastructure.\n\n Functional Descriptions\n1. Data Management:\n   - Store and manage account, client, and transaction information.\n   - Track loan details, including amount, duration, and status.\n\n2. Predictive Modeling:\n   - Analyze historical loan data to train predictive models.\n   - Provide predictions on loan status (A: successful, B: unsuccessful) at initiation.\n\n3. User Interface:\n   - Enable users to input loan parameters and receive predictions.\n   - Display detailed account and transaction history for analysis.\n\n4. Reporting:\n   - Generate reports on prediction accuracy and loan performance.\n   - Facilitate trend analysis and risk assessment.\n\n5. Integration:\n   - Seamlessly integrate with existing financial management systems.\n\n Non-Functional Descriptions\n- Performance: The system should provide predictions quickly (within seconds) to support real-time decision-making.\n- Scalability: Capable of handling a large volume of loans and transactions efficiently.\n- Security: Ensure data privacy and protection, especially sensitive financial and personal information.\n- Reliability: Provide consistent and reliable predictions with minimal downtime.\n- Usability: Intuitive interface for users with varying technical expertise.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_deepseek/0107.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0107\nCREATE TABLE `district` (\n  `district_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `A2` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `A3` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `A4` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `A5` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `A6` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `A7` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `A8` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `A9` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `A10` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `A11` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `A12` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `A13` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `A14` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `A15` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `A16` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`district_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `account` (\n  `account_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `district_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `frequency` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `date` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`account_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `account_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`district_id`) REFERENCES `district` (`district_id`) ON DELETE NO ACTION ON UPDATE NO ACTION\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `client` (\n  `client_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `gender` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `birth_date` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `district_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`client_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `client_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`district_id`) REFERENCES `district` (`district_id`) ON DELETE NO ACTION ON UPDATE NO ACTION\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `card` (\n  `card_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `disp_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `type` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `issued` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`card_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `card_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`disp_id`) REFERENCES `disp` (`disp_id`) ON DELETE NO ACTION ON UPDATE NO ACTION\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `disp` (\n  `disp_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `client_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `account_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `type` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`disp_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `disp_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`client_id`) REFERENCES `client` (`client_id`) ON DELETE NO ACTION ON UPDATE NO ACTION,\n  CONSTRAINT `disp_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`account_id`) REFERENCES `account` (`account_id`) ON DELETE NO ACTION ON UPDATE NO ACTION\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `loan` (\n  `loan_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `account_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `date` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `amount` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `duration` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `payments` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`loan_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `loan_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`account_id`) REFERENCES `account` (`account_id`) ON DELETE NO ACTION ON UPDATE NO ACTION\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `order` (\n  `order_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `account_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `bank_to` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `account_to` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `amount` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `k_symbol` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`order_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `order_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`account_id`) REFERENCES `account` (`account_id`) ON DELETE NO ACTION ON UPDATE NO ACTION\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `trans` (\n  `trans_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `account_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `date` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `type` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `operation` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `amount` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `balance` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `k_symbol` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `bank` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `account` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`trans_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `trans_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`account_id`) REFERENCES `account` (`account_id`) ON DELETE NO ACTION ON UPDATE NO ACTION\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
